WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.080
There are times when a ShellCheck finding
does not require a code change.

2
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:12.984
A script might intentionally rely on a
specific shell behavior,

3
00:00:13.064 --> 00:00:15.977
use a controlled input pattern,

4
00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:21.342
or preserve compatibility with an existing
environment.

5
00:00:21.422 --> 00:00:26.060
That does not mean every warning should be
ignored.

6
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:33.583
It means exceptions should be handled with
discipline so the tool remains useful.

7
00:00:33.663 --> 00:00:41.105
A defensible suppression is narrow,
documented, and close to the code it affects.

8
00:00:41.185 --> 00:00:48.735
The explanation should tell a reviewer why
the behavior is intentional and why it is

9
00:00:48.815 --> 00:00:51.404
acceptable in this context.

10
00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:59.142
A future maintainer should not have to
guess whether the team understood the warning

11
00:00:59.222 --> 00:01:02.674
or simply wanted the pipeline to pass.

12
00:01:02.754 --> 00:01:08.039
The more sensitive or production-critical
the script is,

13
00:01:08.119 --> 00:01:12.326
the more important that explanation
becomes.

14
00:01:12.406 --> 00:01:15.750
Blanket ignores weaken the process.

15
00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:21.978
If a repository disables broad classes of
findings without review,

16
00:01:22.058 --> 00:01:27.990
future problems may be hidden alongside
the original exception.

17
00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:32.493
The signal from ShellCheck becomes less
useful,

18
00:01:32.573 --> 00:01:37.211
and teams gradually stop trusting the lint
result.

19
00:01:37.291 --> 00:01:44.841
Suppression should preserve signal by
quieting only the specific warning that has

20
00:01:44.921 --> 00:01:47.510
been reviewed and accepted.

21
00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:52.120
Accepted findings should be revisited over
time.

22
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:59.427
A suppression that was reasonable for a
legacy migration may no longer be needed

23
00:01:59.507 --> 00:02:02.420
after the script is refactored.

24
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:09.942
A controlled input assumption may become
invalid when the script is reused in a new

25
00:02:10.022 --> 00:02:10.885
context.

26
00:02:10.965 --> 00:02:18.515
Review discipline keeps exceptions from
becoming permanent blind spots and helps

27
00:02:18.595 --> 00:02:24.420
ShellCheck remain a practical aid rather
than a noisy obstacle.
